  "text": "Veterinary Radiation Oncology – Flint Animal Cancer Center\nSkip to content\ncolorado state\nuniversity\nCSU\nflint animal\ncancer center\nAbout Us\nOur Story\nFACC Team\nContact Us\nNews\nFor Owners\nOur Services\nCancer Information Request\nAppointments\nTreatment Options\nPreparing for Your Visit\nFrequently Asked Questions\nFor DVMs\nConsult Line\nTreatment Options\nOne Cure\nAbout One Cure\nRacing for One Cure\nDonate to One Cure\nClinical Trials\nAbout Clinical Trials\nCurrent Clinical Trials\nEducation & Research\nComparative Oncology Research\nOncology Specialty Training\nDonate\nGreatest Need\nPatient Assistance\nOne Cure\nScholarship\nResearch\nFeline Cancer Research\nFACC Friends\nmenu\nclose\nTag:\nVeterinary Radiation Oncology\nsearch\nread more\nLatest News\nRadiation Oncology Team Exploring New Option to Treat Superficial Tumors in Pets\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMarch 4, 2021\nWith technology borrowed from human medicine, the radiation oncology team at the Flint Animal Cancer Center recently treated its first pet patient using superficial radiation therapy. The new radiation machine, originally acquired through a collaboration with the James L. Voss Teaching Hospital’s ophthalmology service to treat squamous cell carcinomas on the eyelids of horses, is […]\nread more\nFACC Patient Stories\nHappy Labrador finds new best friend during treatment for canine cancer\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMarch 11, 2022\nSig O’Rourke, a spunky black Labrador retriever, also known as Mr. Personality, joined Kathy and Pat O’Rourkes’ home when he was five months old. The couple has had many dogs over the years, but never a male, and they weren’t quite sure what to expect. “He’s the sweetest, best dog we’ve ever had,” said Kathy. […]\nread more\nPet Cancer Treatment\n3-D Printed Bolus: Setting A New Standard In Radiation Oncology\nLauren Mingus\n|\nMay 29, 2019\nThe Radiation Oncology service at the Flint Animal Cancer Center is known for breaking new ground. They were the first veterinary oncology service in the U.S. to employ a linear accelerator to treat companion animals and the first in the world to offer stereotactic radiation therapy for pets. Under the direction of Dr. Del Leary, […]\nbrowse articles by topic\nFACC Patient Stories\nLatest News\nOne Cure Clinical Trials\nOne Cure Patients\nOur Team\nPet Cancer Treatment\nResearch\nStories of Hope\nTeaching and Learning\nbrowse articles by tag\nBone Cancer\nBrain Tumor\nCancer Research\nCancer Stories\nCanine Cancer\nCanine Cancer Clinical Trials\nCanine Cancer Prevention Vaccine\ncanine clinical trial\nCanine Hemangiosarcoma\nCanine Leg Amputation\nCanine Lymphoma\nCanine Nasal Tumor\nCanine Osteosarcoma\nCanine Soft Tissue Sarcoma canine cancer clinical trials\nCaring for pets with cancer\ncat cancer\nChemotherapy\ncomparative oncology\nComparative Oncology Research\nDan Regan\nDiagnostic Imaging\nDiet for Pets with Cancer\nEmotional Support\nfeline cancer\nFeline GI Lymphoma\nFeline Lymphoma\nFlint Animal Cancer Center Staff\nGlioma\nGolden Retriever Lifetime Study\nHemangiosarcoma\nLaiks Fund\nlimb-spare surgery\nNasal Osteosarcoma\nNews\nOne Cure\nOne Health\nPatient Assistance Funds\nPatient Care\nPatient Stories\nPatient Story\nPet Cancer\nPet Cancer Comparative Oncology\nPet Cancer Patient Assistance Funds\nPet Cancer Prevention\nPet Cancer Terms\nPet Cancer Treatment\nPet Grief Counseling\npet leg amputation\nPet Loss\nPet Quality of Life\nPlasmacytomas\nRacing for One Cure\nradiation biology\nRadiation Bolus\nRadiation Oncology Research\nRadiation Therapy\nradioimmunology\nResearch\nSoft Tissue Sarcoma\nSpeeding up the Cure\nSurgery\nVeterinary Oncology\nVeterinary Oncology History\nVeterinary Oncology Specialist Training\nVeterinary Oncology Specialty Training\nVeterinary Oncology Training\nVeterinary Radiation Oncology\nWarning Signs of Pet Cancer\nPatient Care Quicklinks\nAppointments\nOur Services\nTreatment Options\nFrequently Asked Questions\nPreparing for Your Visit